  "strategy": "This approach formulation addresses the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by optimizing a step function h(x) defined on [0,2] with integral 1, minimizing the maximum correlation overlap with its complement (1-h). The key idea is to use a multi-resolution gradient descent strategy that:\n\n1) Starts from the provided parent solution and uses interpolation to transfer to higher-resolution grids\n2) Applies gradient-based optimization via SLSQP within each grid to minimize the maximum overlap integral (C)\n3) Uses analytical gradients to maintain optimization performance despite the non-convexity of the problem\n4) Iterates through increasing grid resolutions until either user-defined or time-based stopping criteria are exceeded\n5) Maintains solution feasibility through careful constraint handling\n\nThe mathematical formulation contains a piecewise defined function h(x), where the objective is the maximum shifted correlation over all offsets k. The constraints are that h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x and \u222b h(x) dx = 1, which translate to sum(h) = n_points/2 for the discrete representation.\n\nImplementation strategies include:\n- Using `np.interp` for grid interpolation between resolutions\n- Computing analytical gradients to replace numerical differentiation\n- Employing scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP method for performance\n- Dynamically transferring parent solutions to higher resolutions\n- Handling augment lagged correlations for gradient computation\n\nThe algorithm systematically improves upon the parent solution by:\n1) Conducting rigorous constraint enforcement\n2) Iteratively enhancing the negative average overlap\n3) Balancing gradient calculation complexity with optimization performance\n4) Controlling runtime through resolution doubling and budget regulation\n\nStopping criteria include reaching a maximum resolutionwhere gradient computations become prohibitively expensive.\n\nImplementation details handle:\n```python\nspecify detailed implementation using scipy and numpy",
